Feature: Sending plans
  An [[agent plan]] tells the plane what to do for the next few seconds. It has a
  [[primitive]], a target or a point, a plan id and a time to live. The ground sends it as a
  MAVLink `COMMAND_INT` with the command 31010 (`MAV_CMD_USER_1`). `param1` is the primitive,
  `param2` the target, `param3` the plan id and `param4` the [[time to live]] in seconds.

  `x` and `y` are the latitude and the longitude of the point, in 1e-7 degrees. `z` holds two
  numbers: the altitude above home, to 0.25 m, and the radius, to 1 m. Encode `z` as
  round((altitude + 512) × 4) × 1024 + round(radius). The altitude can be from -512 m to
  511.75 m, and the radius from 0 m to 1023 m. The encoder clamps a value outside its range. In
  Rust, `cognition_plan::WirePlan::to_command` does this.

  The plane answers each plan with a `COMMAND_ACK`. If the plane refuses the plan, the reason
  is in `result_param2`: 1 if the plane is not in [[AGENT mode]], 2 if the plan is malformed,
  3 if the plane has no home yet, and 4 if the plane has no [[executor]].

  The ground sends a new plan about once a second, with a new plan id each time. The plane
  flies the newest plan. If no new plan comes before the time to live ends, the plane circles
  where it is. Thus a plane that loses the ground does not fly away.

  In AGENT mode, the plane sends its [[agent status]], four `NAMED_VALUE_INT` messages, once a
  second. AG_PLAN is
  the id of its plan. AG_PRIM is the primitive of that plan. AG_ENV counts the steps where the
  [[safety envelope]] changed what the plan asked for. AG_FALL tells you if the plane stopped
  the plan, and why.

  Expectation: AGENT mode takes a go-to plan
    Given the flight mode of the plane is AGENT
    When the ground sends a go-to plan
    Then the reply to the plan is accepted
    And the reported plan goes up by 1
    And the reported primitive is go to
    And the time to live of the plan is 600 s

  Expectation: AGENT mode takes a short plan
    Docs: hidden
    Given the flight mode of the plane is AGENT
    When the ground sends a short go-to plan
    Then the reply to the plan is accepted
    And the reported plan goes up by 1
    And the reported primitive is go to
    And the time to live of the plan is 5 s

  Expectation: A plan is refused outside AGENT mode
    The plane takes plans only in AGENT mode. In FBWA, or in any other mode, the pilot or
    ArduPilot flies the plane. Switch to AGENT first. Then send the plan.

    Given the flight mode of the plane is FBWA
    When the ground sends a go-to plan
    Then the reply to the plan is refused for the mode
    And the reported plan stays the same

  Expectation: A malformed plan is refused with a reason
    The plane checks each field of the plan. A primitive that does not exist, a number that
    is not finite or a point off the globe makes the plan malformed. The plane keeps the plan
    that it flies.

    Given the flight mode of the plane is AGENT
    When the ground sends a malformed plan
    Then the reply to the plan is refused as malformed
    And the reported plan stays the same

  Expectation: AGENT mode starts with no plan
    In AGENT mode, the plane forgets the old plan and the old count of AG_ENV. It
    flies wings level and holds its height until a plan comes.

    Given the flight mode of the plane is FBWA
    When the flight mode of the plane is AGENT
    Then the reported plan is 0
    And the reported primitive is none

  Expectation: A new plan id replaces the plan
    The plane does not finish the old plan first. It turns to the new plan at once.

    Given the reported primitive is go to
    When the ground sends an orbit plan
    Then the reported plan goes up by 1
    And the reported primitive is orbit

  Expectation: A repeated plan id changes nothing
    The plane flies a plan id only once. If a plan comes again with the same id, the plane
    keeps the plan that it has, even if the new message has another primitive. Thus a plan
    that the link sends twice does not restart.

    The rule below is what the plane must do. It does not do it yet. The plane flies the first
    plan, but AG_PRIM shows the primitive of the new message (`prim=4`). Until this is
    repaired, do not use AG_PRIM to see what the plane flies after a repeated plan id.

    Given the reported primitive is go to
    When the ground sends the plan id again with an orbit
    Then the reply to the plan is accepted
    And the reported plan stays the same
    And the reported primitive is go to

  Expectation: A plan that runs out makes the plane circle
    If the time to live of the plan ends before a new plan comes, the plane circles where
    it is. It tells the ground why, with AG_FALL and the message "Agent: fallback 1". A short
    [[recovery]] can also show, as `recoveries` in the console. To give
    each plan more time, set `AGENT_GRACE`. It is in seconds, and it is 0 by default.

    Given the time to live of the plan is 5 s
    When the plane flies on for 10 seconds
    Then the reported fallback is plan expired

  Expectation: A new plan ends the circle
    The plane flies the new plan at once. With the parameters of `ardupilot/t1s.parm`, a short
    [[recovery]] can show in AG_FALL as 3 at the same time.

    Given the time to live of the plan is 5 s
    When the ground sends a go-to plan
    Then the reported fallback is none or recovery

  Expectation: The plane reports its plan once a second
    The plane sends AG_PLAN, AG_PRIM, AG_ENV and AG_FALL once a second while it is in AGENT
    mode. Count the AG_PLAN messages to see that the plane is alive.

    Given the flight mode of the plane is AGENT
    When the operator watches the status for 10 seconds
    Then the status reports in 10 seconds are between 9 and 11
